# Caddy site blocks for the three boards — reference copy.
#
# The live file is /srv/caddy/Caddyfile on the deployment host,
# which also serves unrelated sites, so this is not imported from anywhere: it is
# the version-controlled record of what those three blocks say and why. It lived
# on exactly one machine until 2026-08-21, where nothing versioned it and nothing
# would have noticed it being edited.
#
# Append to that host's Caddyfile, then:
#   docker exec caddy caddy validate --config /etc/caddy/Caddyfile --adapter caddyfile
#   docker exec caddy caddy reload   --config /etc/caddy/Caddyfile --adapter caddyfile
#
# The `*.internal.example` hostnames and the /srv path are PLACEHOLDERS — this is
# a public repository. Substitute the real ones when appending to the live file;
# scripts/assert_no_estate_identifiers.py fails the gate if they are committed.
#
# `:80` is deliberate and NOT a missing TLS config. That deployment sits behind a
# proxied Cloudflare record, so the browser gets HTTPS and Caddy is the origin.
# Removing `:80` makes Caddy try ACME for a hostname it does not control the
# resolution of. Read the note on the /live header before changing anything.

# ── hookstack boards ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Three read-only dashboards. Each service already guards its data endpoints with
# a token the page prompts for and keeps in localStorage, so Caddy is a plain
# proxy here and deliberately does NOT inject that token: injecting it would make
# the gate open for everyone who reaches the hostname, and then a second secret
# would be needed to replace the one just neutered.
#
# The rule below each site is an ALLOWLIST. A denylist over 72 endpoints across
# three services is one forgotten path away from publishing an ingest door.
#
# What is deliberately NOT exposed, on any of them:
#   * the ingest doors (/hook/*, /events, /hooks/*). Their callers — the
#     platform, the lark bridge, the judges — all live on this host and reach
#     each other on 127.0.0.1. `judge-notify` in particular is UNSIGNED, being an
#     in-network hop, so publishing it would let anyone post a forged verdict
#     straight to a private Feishu chat.
#   * every write on hookprobe. It approves remediations and writes skills and
#     memory, which are the things the whole design withholds from its own agent.

relay.internal.example:80 {
	@live path /live
	header @live {
		X-Accel-Buffering no
		Cache-Control no-cache
	}
	@board path / /status /live /healthz
	handle @board {
		reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8100 {
			flush_interval -1
		}
	}
	respond 403
}

judge.internal.example:80 {
	# /judgements/*/label is a write, and it is here on purpose: it is the review
	# queue, where one click is one eval label. Making that cheap is the point.
	# Cloudflare buffers a streamed response unless it is told not to. Direct to
	# the origin, /live yields {"type":"hello"} immediately; through the proxy,
	# nothing arrived in ten seconds, so both boards would have loaded once and
	# then sat frozen — the wake-up line is how they stop polling.
	@live path /live
	header @live {
		X-Accel-Buffering no
		Cache-Control no-cache
	}
	@board path / /status /live /healthz /disagreements /labels/export
	@label path_regexp ^/judgements/[^/]+/label$
	handle @board {
		reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8200 {
			flush_interval -1
		}
	}
	handle @label {
		reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8200
	}
	respond 403
}

probe.internal.example:80 {
	# Read the console; adopt nothing. Every route here is bearer-guarded, but the
	# same /v1 prefix carries `PUT /v1/skills/{name}`, `PUT /v1/memory` and
	# `POST /v1/remediations/{id}/approve` — the writes this whole design withholds
	# from its own agent — so the proxy sorts them by what they DO, not by method.
	#
	# GET-only was the first rule and it was too blunt: it 403'd
	# `POST /v1/runs/{key}/distill`, which is a POST that writes nothing. Its own
	# docstring says "returned, never saved" — it assembles a draft from the run
	# record for a person to read, and the save is a separate PUT. A console button
	# that silently does nothing is worse than one that is absent.
	#
	# Allowed below: reads, drafts, and DECLINES. The asymmetry is the point — the
	# gates exist to stop something being adopted, not to stop it being turned
	# down, so rejecting a draft or dismissing a suggestion is safe in a way that
	# accepting one is not.
	@safe_post {
		method POST
		path_regexp ^/v1/(runs/[^/]+/distill|skills/[^/]+/(review|proposal/reject)|memory/suggestions/[^/]+/dismiss|remediations/[^/]+/reject)$
	}
	@stop {
		method POST
		path_regexp ^/sessions/[^/]+/stop$
	}
	@readonly {
		method GET
		not path /hooks/*
	}
	handle @readonly {
		reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8088 {
			flush_interval -1
		}
	}
	handle @safe_post {
		reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8088
	}
	handle @stop {
		reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8088
	}
	# Everything else, named so the list is readable rather than inferred:
	# /hooks/* (signed ingress, callers are on this host), accepting a memory
	# suggestion, approving a remediation or a consolidation, and every PUT or
	# DELETE under /v1. Accepting is done from the Feishu card, which is verified
	# and which you must have been sent.
	respond 403
}
